injury sustained in the harvard-yale  game prevented him from commissioning as  a Marine Corps officer he founded the  Warriors scholar project to help prepare  veterans for academic success in the  liberal arts I first met him three and a  half years ago a few days after I left  the army I was attending the warrior  scholar project at Harvard before I  matriculated at the University of Oregon  in the fall the program he had developed  along with Chris Howell was meant to  teach critical reading and academic  writing through Tocqueville and  Thucydides but it was really so much  more my time there showed me that I was  academically capable of more than I'd  imagined and Jessie and Chris inspired  me to apply it to Yale as an Eli Whitney  student that next year if I hadn't  attended his program I truly believed  that I would not be standing here today  as a senior at Yale  [Applause]  thanks Jared for that kind introduction  I do have one bone to pick it's the Yale  Harvard game not the harvard-yale game  but other than that it's much  appreciated thanks Jared now I feel  especially humbled to receive this honor  here today because for me  Yale is where it all started I had no  intention to join the military before I  arrived at Yale but it didn't take me  long to realize that if a guy like me  could be plucked out of a hardscrabble  Midwestern town and given an opportunity  to attend Yale University then America  truly must be the land of opportunity I  learned about the foundations of  democracy and the brilliance of the  Federalist Papers at Yale but the moral  lessons were at least equally as  important as the academic ones the Yale  Creed for God for country and for Yale  resonated deeply with me I stood in awe  as I ran my fingers across the names  inscribed in the walls just outside the  doors of those who paid the ultimate  sacrifice and as I viewed the Cenotaph  on by Niki Plaza my knees buckled under  the weight of everything that those had  fought on our behalf had secured it was  Yale that compelled me to say send me I  need to do my part now ultimately as  Jared alluded to it was not part of the  plan for me to serve my country in the  military while tackling the running back  of that school in Cambridge that shall  remain unnamed I tore two nerves in my  neck which left parts of my right arm  and shoulder permanently paralyzed now  if I had to do it all over again I'd  give my entire arm for this place but  losing the Marine Corps as a result of  my injury  left in me a void that I relentlessly  try to fill every hour of every day  fortunately Yale equipped me to serve my  country in ways that could help fill  that void we ran our first warrior  scholar project academic boot camp here  at Yale in 2012 serving nine veterans  and we've now put over 700 veterans and  Counting through our academic boot camps  at more than 15 campuses across the  country during the warrior scholar  project veterans like Rob and Jared  wrestle with classical texts and engage  with some of the world's greatest minds  on themes of liberty equality and  democracy they realized that a deeper  love for one's country is achieved  through critical appreciation rather  than blind veneration and they realized  that here at home they can still live in  pursuit of the principles and ideals  that they put their lives on the line to  defend overseas the warrior scholars  like Jared and Rob they leave the  program not just ready to become great  students but prepared to live up to our  highest hopes and dreams for them to  become members of the next greatest  generation who remember not just for the  courage with which they fought but for  what they accomplished after they  returned home now I couldn't be more  grateful for the Yale Family which is  which has provided steadfast support for  our mission Yale family members Sam  Chauncey Nick Brady Charles Johnson  Glenn Greenberg Mary Beth and Peter  Oppenheimer were early believers in our  mission and their incredible support  continues through this day Yale  professors Norma Thompson and Marla  gajja John Gaddis Mike photos and many  others helped shape the minds of these  warrior scholars and my co-founder and  Yale classmate Chris Howell is one of  the most talented and dynamic people  with whom I've had the privilege  work and of course people like Jared and  Rob give us all hope for the future  thank you again for this honor  [Applause]  ladies and gentlemen it is military  tradition to stand as I read the award  citation please join me attention to  orders citation to accompany the gold  Valor award to cadet Amanda e Lloyd  on 9 July 2017 cadet Amanda e Lloyd and  three friends we're at Lake Ontario  Beach when they heard screams for help  coming from five women and children in  rough water where the South Sandy Creek  enters the lake  cadet lloyd and her companions acted  quickly to organize a rescue attempt and  alert authorities to the danger in  response to hearing cries of we are  going to die from swimmers who were 200  yards off shore and struggling and ten  foot high waves strong current and  undertow cadet lloyd entered the water  with two lifejackets swimming to a  struggling young girl and taking her in  tow knowing her fellow rescuer had  entered the water without a life jacket  but unable to see him in the high waves  cadet Lloyd located him by calling his  name upon swimming to his location she  found him holding two additional young  girls using both lifejackets  cadet Lloyd and the other rescuer fought  against the undertow and brought the  three distressed swimmers to shore where  a third rescuer was waiting  cadet Lloyd and her companion re-entered  the water with the lifejackets swimming  to the two adult women several hundred  yards from shore  each rescuer took one adult in tow and  began to swim to shore joined by a third  rescuer in out they managed to get the  two to  women to safety five women and children  are alive today due to the quick and  selfless actions of cadet Lloyd by her  prompt actions and humanitarian regard  for her fellow human  cadet Lloyd has reflected great credit  upon herself in the United States Air  Force  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  [Applause]  let us pray eternal God we give thanks  for those who gave their lives in the  struggle for peace and justice  we are grateful that there are men and  women in our world who struggle for such  noble ideals and now we would dedicate  ourselves to ensure that their sacrifice  was not in vain as we struggle to make  our world a better place for all people  to live we beseech you to give us the  strength we need as we seek to fulfill  your will amen  well everybody that concludes our  ceremony for today thank you again so  much for coming and to all of our  speakers and congratulations again to  those of you who were tributes and  awards as well many thanks to the  planning committee and to our ceremony  participants as I said including Tom  Duffy and the Yale School of Music brass  band quartet  and again I just call your attention to  the other programs that will go on today  but again have a great day and thank you  for coming  [Music]
 

